{{! ─── Standards Foundation ───────────────────────────────────────────── }}
<section class="sw-section sw-standards">
  <div class="sw-container">
    <h2 class="sw-section-title">Built on what no platform can take away.</h2>
    <p class="sw-section-subtitle">
      HTTP, JSON:API, SPARQL, Docker. Open standards owned by no single company,
      usable by everyone. We've seen what happens when a platform owns the protocol —
      it works until it doesn't. These ones, nobody owns.
    </p>
    <div class="sw-standards-grid">
      <div class="sw-card sw-card--sky sw-standard-card">
        <div class="sw-card-icon">HTTP</div>
        <h3>HTTP</h3>
        <p>The universal transport. Every microservice speaks it. Every team already knows it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-card sw-card--sky sw-standard-card">
        <div class="sw-card-icon">JSON</div>
        <h3>JSON:API</h3>
        <p>Consistent, predictable APIs across every service. No custom contracts to negotiate.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-card sw-card--sky sw-standard-card">
        <div class="sw-card-icon">SPQL</div>
        <h3>SPARQL</h3>
        <p>Query your entire data graph with a single, powerful standard. No joins. No migrations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-card sw-card--sky sw-standard-card">
        <div class="sw-card-icon">🐳</div>
        <h3>Docker</h3>
        <p>Every microservice runs in isolation. Scale exactly what you need, when you need it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="sw-section-note">
      Any language. Any tool. Any team. Nothing here asks for loyalty — because nothing here can be taken away.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{{! ─── The Journey ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── }}
<section class="sw-section sw-section--sky sw-journey">
  <div class="sw-container">
    <h2 class="sw-section-title">A transitional architecture.</h2>
    <p class="sw-section-subtitle">
      Most teams start with a monolith. semantic.works takes you to the next step —
      microservices with a shared semantic model — and gradually from there into
      fully distributed applications. No big bang rewrite. Each step stands on its own.
    </p>

    <div class="sw-journey-steps">
      <div class="sw-journey-step">
        <div class="sw-journey-dot">1</div>
        <h3>Monolith</h3>
        <p>One big application. Works today. Hard to change. Costly to scale. The place most teams start.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-journey-step">
        <div class="sw-journey-dot">2</div>
        <h3>Semantic layer</h3>
        <p>Microservices that share a data model. No integration chaos. Each service does one thing well, in any language.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-journey-step">
        <div class="sw-journey-dot">3</div>
        <h3>Sovereign data</h3>
        <p>Distributed. Linked. Queryable from anywhere. You own it. No vendor holds the key.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="sw-journey-note">
      40+ applications in production. Governments, public services, enterprises, and
      researchers have walked this path — and their data is still theirs.
    </p>

    <div class="sw-contributors">
      <span class="sw-contributors-label">In production</span>
      <span class="sw-contributor">Kaleidos</span>
      <span class="sw-contributor">LBLOD</span>
      <span class="sw-contributor">Veeakker.be</span>
      <span class="sw-contributor">YourDataStories</span>
      <span class="sw-contributor">Big Data Europe</span>
      <span class="sw-contributor">ESCO · European Commission</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{{! ─── The Five Pillars ───────────────────────────────────────────────── }}
<section class="sw-section">
  <div class="sw-container">
    <div class="sw-pillars-intro">
      <h2 class="sw-section-title">One command. The hard problems are already solved.</h2>
      <p class="sw-section-subtitle">
        <code>docker-compose up</code> and you have routing, sessions, a full JSON:API,
        security, and a triplestore. Weeks of infrastructure work — already built,
        already working. Now write the thing only you can write.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-pillars-grid">
      <div class="sw-card sw-card--red sw-pillar">
        <div class="sw-card-icon">id</div>
        <div class="sw-card-title">identifier</div>
        <p class="sw-card-body">
          Every request gets a session. The entry point that makes your architecture coherent.
        </p>
        <a href="https://github.com/mu-semtech/mu-identifier" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sw-card-link">View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-card sw-card--red sw-pillar">
        <div class="sw-card-icon">→</div>
        <div class="sw-card-title">dispatcher</div>
        <p class="sw-card-body">
          Routes every request to the right microservice. Simple rules, powerful routing.
        </p>
        <a href="https://github.com/mu-semtech/mu-dispatcher" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sw-card-link">View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-card sw-card--red sw-pillar">
        <div class="sw-card-icon">res</div>
        <div class="sw-card-title">mu-cl-resources</div>
        <p class="sw-card-body">
          Describe your data model, get a full JSON:API. No code. Just configuration.
        </p>
        <a href="https://github.com/mu-semtech/mu-cl-resources" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sw-card-link">View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-card sw-card--red sw-pillar">
        <div class="sw-card-icon">🔒</div>
        <div class="sw-card-title">sparql-parser</div>
        <p class="sw-card-body">
          Security lives in one place: the database. Every service sees only what the user is allowed to see — automatically, without any service needing to implement it.
        </p>
        <a href="https://github.com/mu-semtech/sparql-parser" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sw-card-link">View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-card sw-card--red sw-pillar">
        <div class="sw-card-icon">db</div>
        <div class="sw-card-title">Virtuoso</div>
        <p class="sw-card-body">
          The triplestore where your linked data lives. Proven, performant, standards-compliant.
        </p>
        <a href="https://github.com/redpencilio/docker-virtuoso" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="sw-card-link">View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{{! ─── Out of the Box ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── }}
<section class="sw-section sw-section--sky">
  <div class="sw-container">
    <div class="sw-services-intro">
      <h2 class="sw-section-title">Don't build what's already solved.</h2>
      <p class="sw-section-subtitle">
        The things every application needs are already built, tested in production, and
        ready to drop in. Pick what you need. Leave the rest.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-services-grid">
      <div class="sw-service-item">
        <div class="sw-service-dot"></div>
        <div>
          <p class="sw-service-name">Authorization</p>
          <p class="sw-service-desc">Fine-grained, auditable access control through SPARQL. Every query filtered at the database level.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-service-item">
        <div class="sw-service-dot"></div>
        <div>
          <p class="sw-service-name">Login services</p>
          <p class="sw-service-desc">OAuth, username/password, or mock-login for development. Swap strategies without touching your app.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-service-item">
        <div class="sw-service-dot"></div>
        <div>
          <p class="sw-service-name">mu-search</p>
          <p class="sw-service-desc">Full-text search over your graph, powered by ElasticSearch. Index what you need, search the rest.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-service-item">
        <div class="sw-service-dot"></div>
        <div>
          <p class="sw-service-name">File service</p>
          <p class="sw-service-desc">Upload, download, store. Wired into your semantic model from day one.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-service-item">
        <div class="sw-service-dot"></div>
        <div>
          <p class="sw-service-name">Push updates</p>
          <p class="sw-service-desc">Long-running job? Create a task, let the frontend watch it live — about 10 lines of frontend code. No WebSocket server. No polling loop. We handle the transport.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-service-item">
        <div class="sw-service-dot"></div>
        <div>
          <p class="sw-service-name">mu-cl-resources</p>
          <p class="sw-service-desc">Any entity — products, people, documents — listed, created, and updated via JSON:API. Zero code.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-service-item">
        <div class="sw-service-dot"></div>
        <div>
          <p class="sw-service-name">mu-javascript-template</p>
          <p class="sw-service-desc">Start a new microservice in JavaScript in minutes. Access rights handled automatically via sparql-parser.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-service-item">
        <div class="sw-service-dot"></div>
        <div>
          <p class="sw-service-name">mu-python-template</p>
          <p class="sw-service-desc">The same, in Python. One command, a running service with access rights handled automatically.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-service-item">
        <div class="sw-service-dot"></div>
        <div>
          <p class="sw-service-name">mu-cli</p>
          <p class="sw-service-desc">Maintenance scripts that ship with your application — add an admin user, reset an index. Each command runs in a sandbox: even a compromised dependency can't touch anything outside the project.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-service-item">
        <div class="sw-service-dot"></div>
        <div>
          <p class="sw-service-name">Audit trail</p>
          <p class="sw-service-desc">Every call through the stack traced back to the session that initiated it. Stored in Elasticsearch, visualised in Kibana. Encrypted on disk — recoverable with a GPG key you keep offline.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="sw-services-more">
      And <a href="https://github.com/mu-semtech" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">200+ community repositories</a>
      on GitHub — services, templates, and tooling built by the community.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{{! ─── Governance ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── }}
<section class="sw-section sw-governance">
  <div class="sw-container">
    <h2 class="sw-section-title">A stable platform to build on.</h2>
    <div class="sw-governance-body">
      <p>
        Quality software needs a stable foundation — not a new paradigm every few months.
        semantic.works evolves gradually, steered by a core team with decades of experience
        and shaped by a broad community.
      </p>
      <p>
        The features are orthogonal by design — logging, authorization, and caching work
        without your services knowing about them. One consistent mental model, from your
        first service to your fiftieth. When the framework evolves, the path forward is
        always visible from where you are. And if we ever stop, MIT means it's yours —
        to use, to fork, to carry forward.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-governance-pillars">
      <span class="sw-gov-pill">Gradual, careful evolution</span>
      <span class="sw-gov-pill">Multi-actor steering</span>
      <span class="sw-gov-pill">No single-vendor lock-in</span>
      <span class="sw-gov-pill">EU digital sovereignty</span>
      <span class="sw-gov-pill">Open standards throughout</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

